statement:
You get an integer array `nums` of length `n` and an array `pattern` of length `m` whose entries are each `-1`, `0` or `1`. A subarray of `m + 1` consecutive elements of `nums`, starting at index `i`, **fits** the pattern when, for every `k` from `0` to `m - 1`, the move from `nums[i + k]` to `nums[i + k + 1]` goes the way `pattern[k]` says:

- `1`: the next element is strictly larger;
- `0`: the next element is equal;
- `-1`: the next element is strictly smaller.

Return how many subarrays of `nums` fit the pattern.

validator:
function validate([nums, pattern]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(nums) || nums.length < 2 || nums.length > 1e6) return 'nums needs 2 to 10^6 values.';
  if (nums.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 1 || v > 1e9)) return 'Each value of nums is an integer from 1 to 10^9.';
  if (!Array.isArray(pattern) || pattern.length < 1 || pattern.length >= nums.length) return 'pattern needs at least 1 entry and fewer entries than nums.';
  if (pattern.some(v => v !== -1 && v !== 0 && v !== 1)) return 'Each pattern entry is -1, 0 or 1.';
  return null;
}

